# The paper is not yet posted. When SSRN assigns its abstract id, add to
# preferred-citation:
#   url: "https://ssrn.com/abstract=<id>"
#   doi: "10.2139/ssrn.<id>"
# and change its type to article with journal "SSRN Electronic Journal".
preferred-citation:
  type: unpublished
  title: >-
    Same Formula, Different Signal: Funding-Rate Censoring and the Premium
    Gap between Hyperliquid and Centralised Perpetuals
  abstract: >-
    Hyperliquid, Binance and Bybit set perpetual funding by one rule: while
    the averaged premium lies in a 10 bp band, funding equals an interest
    rate of 0.01% per eight hours, and a settlement reveals the premium only
    as that band. From 2 October 2025 to 28 April 2026, 46.0% of
    Hyperliquid's hourly BTC settlements sit at that rate, against 5.9% on
    Binance and 7.7% on Bybit. From 11 December 2023 to 30 September 2026
    the shares are 55.2%, 23.8% and 27.3% for BTC and 52.0%, 23.1% and 28.3%
    for ETH; taken together, the two quarters in which the exchanges'
    premiums were positive reverse the ordering. Matched hour by hour with
    the exchanges' own premium indices, Hyperliquid's BTC premium lies a
    median 2.2 bps above both (ETH 1.7), in four hours out of five and in
    every quarter. For BTC the gap persists but varies: 3.8 bps in 2024, 3.0
    to 3.1 in 2025, 0.3 to 0.5 in 2026Q1 and 2026Q2 and 1.3 to 1.5 in
    2026Q3. By a Shapley split it accounts for about all of the difference
    in shares from 2024Q3 to 2025Q4, but for less than half in 2026Q1 and
    2026Q2 (ETH: 55% to 70%), when all three median premiums lay below the
    band and dispersion mattered more. Cross-venue comparisons of
    positioning should therefore use premiums, with their constructions in
    view, not headline rates. A two-state Markov-switching AR(1) on the
    premium reverts faster under stress only around October 2025:
    allowing for switching, the half-life ratio is 1.5, not significantly
    different from one under a misspecification-robust covariance that one
    hour of the 10 October crash dominates, and later data reverse it. A
    three-state Gaussian hidden Markov model separates hourly volatility
    into 14.6%, 33.3% and 88.4% annualised states and estimates the direct
    move from the high to the low state at zero to three decimals, as do its
    fits to simulated single-regime Student-t GARCH paths. All results are
    descriptive.
  authors:
    - family-names: Albiez
      given-names: Gregor
      email: gregor.albiez@students.fhnw.ch
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6036-4932"
      affiliation: >-
        School of Business, University of Applied Sciences and Arts
        Northwestern Switzerland (FHNW), Basel
  year: 2026
  month: 10
  notes: >-
    Working paper, October 2026.
